From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Paul=20B=C3=BCchner?=
 <45827409+paulbuechner@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 20:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] vtkRenderWindow: Reverse Z

A fixed-point depth buffer that runs from 0 at the near plane to 1 at
the far one resolves less and less toward the far plane, the more so
the larger the ratio of the clipping range: an application that lets
the eye come close to a large model has to keep the near plane out,
and clips what comes nearer. Reversed, floating-point depth, 1 at the
near plane and 0 at the far one in 32-bit floats, resolves about the
same share of any distance.

vtkRenderWindow::UseReverseZ turns it on. An OpenGL window then makes
its render, display and resolve framebuffers with 32-bit float depth
(their blits need one format), clips depth to [0, 1] (glClipControl:
OpenGL 4.5 or ARB_clip_control; without them the window turns it off
and warns), tests GL_GEQUAL and clears depth to 0. vtkOpenGLCamera's
projection maps the near plane to 1 and the far one to 0, and so do
vtkRenderer's conversions between view and display coordinates, so a
depth read from the window goes back to its world point as before. The
polydata mapper's coincident-topology offsets turn round, a unit of the
constant one 1/65000 of the fragment's own depth, as it is of the depth
range in a fixed-point buffer.

What computes, compares or converts depth itself follows in the next
patch.

The new members come last in their classes, so a library built against
the unpatched headers finds every other member where it was.
